fix(scripts): refuse the destructive test packages while the stack is up (#275)

test/failure re-points the transparency log and test/smoke ends its teardown
in `docker compose down -v`. Both are correct on a clean host; five wrapper
call sites reached them without saying so, and the rule written into the
contributor guide after the first incident was broken twice more in two days.

scripts/test-suite.sh is now the one place that decides what the whole suite
is, how a running deployment is recognised, and what each destructive package
does to one. Every wrapper asks it: coverage-floors.sh, test-no-skips.sh, the
Makefile's `test`, `cover` and `smoke` targets and the CI coverage job. On a
host with nothing running it answers `./...`, so CI runs the tree it always
ran and both packages stay reachable.

Reaching either package refuses rather than proceeding and being harmless by
luck: internal/stackguard asks the same script from each TestMain, so a bare
`go test ./test/smoke` is stopped before the first container. The refusal
names what is running, says how to run the package properly, and takes a
named opt-out — teardown-guard's shape, for teardown-guard's reason.

Driven by scripts/test-suite-selftest.sh, watched failing first: every wrapper
shape that reached a destructive package goes red, and the two controls that
keep it from becoming a ban — an ordinary package with the deployment up, and
both destructive packages on a quiet host — stay green. internal/stackguard's
tests were written against an undefined symbol and observed failing for that
reason before the package existed.

